  "reason": "The records collectively make the later approach plausibly reconstructible. R1, R3, R5, and especially R6 establish mechanical alloying/high-energy milling followed by SPS as a route for Ti-containing multicomponent alloys, with R1 and R6 covering short milling times near the later paper's 1–6 h range. R12 supplies a closely related Al-Co-Cr-Fe-Ni-Ti alloy consolidated by SPS for 10 min, yielding a dense, multiphase, high-hardness bulk. R2, R3, R4, R6, R9, R16, and R18 collectively connect grain refinement, accumulated defects or dislocations, solid-solution formation, low porosity, and nanoscale structures with increased hardness or strength. Thus an expert could reasonably combine short HEBM with SPS for CoCrFeNiTi and predict strengthening from nanocrystalline grains, defects, and solid solution effects. The exact 83% FCC/17% BCC balance, low 900–1000 °C SPS window, and specific Ti thermodynamic explanation are not directly disclosed, but they are refinements rather than a wholly new processing concept."
